# mount.pdxfs — caps.decl
#
# Wave: R53 (design/tooling/volume-tooling-ux.md §2.2 + §4 + §9.2, paideia-os
# repo). mount.pdxfs is a standalone CLI tool (a ring-3 ELF with its own
# `_start`, not a shared library like libpdx-volume/libpdx-audit): it
# performs its own syscalls directly (self-contained inlined-syscall
# pattern, matching src/user/true.pdx / cat.pdx / mkdir.pdx and this
# wave's own sibling tool mkfs.pdxfs) rather than delegating every
# kernel touch to a linked library.
#
# consumed_kinds (kernel-owned; paideia-os repo):
#
#   KIND_USER               = 0x190  (src/kernel/core/cap/kind_user.pdx)
#     -- invoker identity, consumed for audit attribution
#        (`invoker_user` field of PdxFsMountRecord@0.1, §7.2) and for
#        the elevate-request identity at M3-001. Not dereferenced by any
#        M1 code path -- M1 never journals, never elevates, and its
#        --dry-run print omits `invoker_user` (see mount_record.pdx
#        module header for exactly which of the ten schema fields M1's
#        four-field slice covers).
#
#   KIND_VOLUME             = 0x1A0  (src/kernel/core/cap/kind_volume.pdx)
#     -- the tool's one required input cap: <volume-cap> from argv
#        resolves to a KIND_VOLUME cap URI (e.g. `cap:volume:0x0002`)
#        looked up in the invoker's cap environment (§4.1). Per §4.2 the
#        real body narrows this to a `mount+query`-scoped sub-cap via
#        libpdx-volume.vol_kind_narrow before calling sys_mount
#        (mount.pdxfs.M2-001). NAMING/SHAPE GAP flagged for main: the
#        landed KIND_VOLUME op catalog (kind_volume.pdx, VOL_OP_QUERY_*
#        ordinals 0..8 + VOL_OP_DEBUG_PRINT=9, VOL_OP_MAX=9) has no
#        `VOL_OP_MOUNT` ordinal -- there is no cap-invoke operation
#        named "mount" to narrow against. §4.2's "mount+query" phrase is
#        this design doc's own shorthand for "the rights band a mount
#        operation needs" (R_VOL_READ|R_VOL_INVOKE at minimum, per
#        kind_volume.pdx's R_VOL_* constants), not a specific op ordinal
#        -- the actual attach-to-mount-point action is expected to be a
#        dedicated `sys_mount` syscall (not yet landed; see design's
#        companion osarch half) taking a KIND_VOLUME cap slot as an
#        argument, not a KIND_VOLUME.cap_invoke call. Confirm with
#        osarch before mount.pdxfs.M2-001 implements a real
#        vol_kind_narrow + sys_mount call pair -- this mirrors the exact
#        shape of the KIND_BLOCK_DEVICE naming gap mkfs.pdxfs's own
#        caps.decl flags for main. Not dereferenced by any M1 code path
#        -- M1's --dry-run path never calls vol_kind_narrow; it only
#        holds the raw argv pointer to <volume-cap>'s URI string and
#        prints it verbatim (see argv.pdx's ParsedArgv.volume_cap_ptr).
#
#   KIND_PDXFS_MOUNT_TABLE  = 0x1A5  (src/kernel/core/cap/kind_pdxfs_mount_table.pdx)
#     -- write authority to append one row after a successful sys_mount
#        (§4.2, mount.pdxfs.M2-002). Not invoked by any M1 code path --
#        M1 never calls sys_mount and so never has a row to append.
#
#   KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL    = 0x191  (src/kernel/core/cap/kind_elevate_channel.pdx)
#     -- STILL a PLACEHOLDER as of the M3-001 (#7) landing. §4.2's
#        mount-point-class table (`/system/**`, `/boot/**`, `/dev/**` --
#        always elevate; cross-user `/home/**` -- founder-only elevate)
#        is now REALLY classified by src/elevate.pdx's `mount_point_
#        class`, and `mount_elev_require_system` is a real, wired call
#        SITE for libpdx-elevate's `elevate_client_acquire` -- but no
#        code path in ANY landing of this repo has ever bound a real
#        KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL broker-endpoint cap (the `parent_ep_slot`
#        libpdx-elevate's own README says is "declared by whatever the
#        caller links this library into, not by libpdx-elevate" itself),
#        so `mount_elev_require_system`'s body is a documented
#        fail-closed STUB returning MOUNT_ELEV_DENY unconditionally --
#        see that file's own module header for the full write-up,
#        mirroring mkfs.pdxfs's own src/elevate_wire.pdx (M3-005, #12)
#        over the identical library and the identical gap.
#
#   KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT       = 5 (base kind, R20b) -- the semantic-pipe
#     stdin/stdout surface. Per design/tooling/r49-r50-plan.md §3.2, a
#     semantic pipe is "a subclass of KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT (base kind 5)
#     carrying a schema handle alongside the byte stream." Declared here
#     as a placeholder per §4.2's own caps list ("KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT --
#     for semantic pipe + audit journal + optional elevate broker");
#     NOT bound or invoked by any M1 code. M1's stdout is a plain
#     `sys_write(1, ...)` of a human-readable line (src/mount_record.pdx);
#     the schema-typed framing over a bound KIND_IPC_ENDPOINT lands at
#     mount.pdxfs.M3-003 once libpdx-semantic-pipe (or an equivalent) is
#     linkable -- see design/architecture.md §4 for the deferral note,
#     mirroring mkfs.pdxfs's own caps.decl for the identical deferral.
#
# declares_output_schemas: none bound at M1. `PdxFsMountRecord@0.1`'s
# FIELD SHAPE is fixed by design/tooling/volume-tooling-ux.md §7.2 and
# this M1 landing's --dry-run path prints a line-based textual rendering
# of exactly four of its ten fields (volume, mount_point, flags,
# result_code -- see src/mount_record.pdx module header for which
# fields M1 omits and why). The typed schema-registry binding
# (BLAKE3-truncated fingerprint constant, semantic-pipe framing) is
# owned by mount.pdxfs.M3-003 per volume-tooling-ux.md §7.4, not this
# issue.
#
# M3 landing status (see STATUS.md, design/architecture.md §11): three
# new satellite-library links, one KIND-shape confirmation (libpdx-
# elevate's, above), no new syscalls of this repo's own.
#
# consumed_satellite_libs (all cross-repo bare-label `call`s, the same
# mechanism M2's `vol_kind_narrow` call already uses -- see src/
# volume_cap.pdx's own "libpdx-volume link status" note):
#
#   libpdx-audit (@0.2, commit bfc63a5) -- `AuditClient::audit_begin`,
#     `AuditClient::audit_commit` (src/audit_wire.pdx, #8). REAL calls
#     (real IPC send attempts, sharing one audit_id between the INTENT
#     event at audit_begin and the RESULT event at audit_commit), but
#     the daemon-side dispatch is confirmed STUBBED (libpdx-audit's own
#     STATUS.md: `audit_journal_broker_dispatch` returns `AJB_DISPATCH_
#     STUB`) -- every call in this landing may therefore no-op at the
#     daemon, per this issue's own forgiving-posture instruction. No
#     `parent_audit_id` parameter exists on `audit_commit` -- see
#     src/audit_wire.pdx's own module header for how this issue's own
#     "parent_audit_id" wording maps onto the real API's shared-audit_id
#     mechanism instead.
#
#   libpdx-elevate (v1.1.0-in-progress, mature) -- NOT actually called
#     for a real elevation decision (src/elevate.pdx's `mount_elev_
#     require_system`, #7): this library's real API (`elevate_client_
#     acquire`/`elevate_client_require`) needs a `parent_ep_slot`
#     broker-endpoint cap this repo does not hold (KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL
#     = 0x191, still the M1 placeholder, unchanged by this landing).
#     `mount_elev_require_system` is therefore a fail-closed stub
#     returning DENY unconditionally -- see that file's own module
#     header for the full mismatch between this issue's assumed API
#     shape and libpdx-elevate's real one (identical gap, identical
#     library, as mkfs.pdxfs's own src/elevate_wire.pdx, M3-005 #12).
#
#   libpdx-semantic-pipe (v1.0.0, real + released) -- NOT linked
#     (src/pipe_wire.pdx, #9): confirmed via GitHub issue paideia-os#2000
#     that `Registry::bind_by_name` is inert (the backing `svc.schema-
#     registry` service does not exist yet, so `bind_by_name` always
#     returns 304/LOOKUP_FAIL). Per this issue's own fallback
#     instruction, every PdxFsMountRecord@0.1 this repo emits remains
#     the M1/M2 line-based `sys_write(1, ...)` rendering, now preceded
#     by one documented deferral header line.
#
# M3-004 (#10) failure-taxonomy note: `src/mount_record.pdx`'s eleven
# new `MR_RESULT_*` codes map every kernel errno `sys_mount` (sysno 75)
# can return -- per `grep -n "pub let SYS_MOUNT_" src/kernel/core/
# syscall/sys_mount.pdx` (paideia-os) -- plus raw `-ENOSYS` (the value
# `dispatch_mount`'s still-unwired stub actually returns today) and four
# policy refusals this repo's own pipeline detects before ever reaching
# a syscall (`MPC_INVALID`, `VC_ERR_BAD_URI`, `VC_ERR_NARROW_FAILED`,
# and the M3-001 elevate-stub outcome itself). None of the six real
# `SYS_MOUNT_*` sentinels are reachable in practice today -- `dispatch_
# mount` (`src/kernel/core/syscall/dispatch.pdx` L1975) is still an
# unconditional `-ENOSYS` stub per `src/mount_op.pdx`'s own KERNEL GAP
# #1 -- but their classification arms are real, wired code kept ready
# for the day that gap closes.
#
# No KIND naming gap between this repo's four consumed kinds and the
# kernel tree as of this landing -- unlike mkfs.pdxfs's KIND_BLOCK_DEVICE
# placeholder and libpdx-volume's KIND_PDXFS_MOUNT_TABLE row-shape gap
# (both flagged in those repos' own caps.decl), all four kinds named
# above (KIND_USER=0x190, KIND_VOLUME=0x1A0, KIND_PDXFS_MOUNT_TABLE=0x1A5,
# KIND_ELEVATE_CHANNEL=0x191) are real, already-landed kernel ordinals
# with matching names at the exact source paths cited. The one real gap
# is the KIND_VOLUME op-catalog mismatch noted above (no VOL_OP_MOUNT
# ordinal), which is a rights-shape question for mount.pdxfs.M2-001, not
# a missing-kind question.
#
# M1 landing status (see STATUS.md): this repo performs exactly two
# syscalls at M1 -- `sys_write` (SC+ ID 1, to fd 1 for the dry-run
# record and fd 2 for error diagnostics) and `sys_exit` (SC+ ID 60). No
# sys_open, no sys_mount, no sys_svc_lookup, no sys_ipc_send, no
# cap-invoke of any kind -- every KIND above is declared for the SHAPE
# this tool's future milestones consume, per the same "typed-against,
# not yet touched" posture mkfs.pdxfs's own M1 caps.decl documents for
# its stub functions.
